      "writtenOn": "2026-09-09T21:37:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not convinced SMALLEST_TYPE is needed. As I understand it, EC_HOST_CMD_HANDLER_REQ_ONLY should be used when the response size varies,  EC_HOST_CMD_HANDLER_RESP_ONLY should be used when the request size varies, and EC_HOST_CMD_HANDLER_UNBOUND should be used when both vary.\n\nFor handlers that support multiple versions the bounds for each version must be checked by that handler. The SMALLEST_TYPE check may even lead developers to assume the response size does not need to be checked in the handler.",
      "revId": "d75cee4ddf838da76a4e17ac24fda41677d3931d",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0e1b84a9_f6503b47",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1530058
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-10T02:39:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e EC_HOST_CMD_HANDLER_REQ_ONLY should be used when the response size varies, EC_HOST_CMD_HANDLER_RESP_ONLY should be used when the request size varies\n\nWith this convention, the upstream macros naming convention is misleading.\n\nIdeally, we should have separate handlers for each version making it clear the expected request/response structs to be used, so the upstream host command handler common processing can reject requests before invoking the handler.  Reducing the amount of boiler plate logic for verifying req/resp sizes in each handler.  I can make that change now, but I fear that would introduce too much change (requiring the STRUCT_SECTION_ITERABLE creating unique names to differentiate the version). \n\nFor now, having an incremental change using a macro that determines the smallest struct type can reduce any human error when calculating it themselves. A future change can then split the handlers.",
      "parentUuid": "72628b2d_483af051",
      "revId": "d75cee4ddf838da76a4e17ac24fda41677d3931d",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
